I"<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Without a good way to detect and mitigate inequalities caused by AI, AI may do more harm than good in healthcare and widen the gaps that already exist. Practitioners lacked a technical tool to quantify these inequalities so they could debug and audit their data and models.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We proposed a generic allocation-deterioration framework. Inequality is quantified as the area between two curves that relate the score a model allocates to patients to how much those patients deteriorate. We tested it on ten synthetic datasets (more than 33,000 patients) made from a real intensive care dataset, HiRID, and then on two real ICU datasets and AI models trained for two resource allocation scenarios.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>The framework detected and quantified inequality in proportion to the amount we built in. Compared with men, women had up to 33% poorer deterioration in markers of prognosis in HiRID ICUs. All four AI models assessed induced significant inequalities (2.45% to 43.2%) for non-White compared with White patients, and the models significantly worsened inequalities already in the data in 3 of 8 assessments, one by more than nine times.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Bias in the data is only part of the picture, because models can add to it. This gave us a way to measure both, and it led to our later funded work on quantifying and mitigating bias in medical AI. The code is open.</p>
